    {
      "id": "PEANUTS",
      "label": "Peanuts",
      "notes": "\"vegetable oil\" is deliberately absent: Canadian labelling requires peanut oil to be declared as peanut, so a generic \"vegetable oil\" is canola, soy or corn and would warn on almost every packaged food. The unqualified nut words stay ambiguous rather than definite, since mixed nuts and nut butters often but not always include peanuts.",
      "terms": [
        "peanut butter", "peanut oil", "peanut flour", "peanut", "groundnut", "ground nut",
        "earth nut", "arachis", "monkey nut", "beer nut", "mandelona", "goober"
      ],
      "ambiguous": [
        "mixed nut", "nut butter", "nut oil", "nut paste", "trail mix", "satay", "nut"
      ],
      "exclusions": ["peanut free"]
    },
    {
      "id": "TREE_NUTS",
      "label": "Tree nuts",
      "notes": "\"hickory nut\", not bare \"hickory\": on a label \"hickory\" is almost always smoke flavouring from the wood. \"coconut\" stays ambiguous because Canada does not treat it as a tree nut while the United States does. \"butternut\" is left out on purpose - butternut squash is far commoner than the nut.",
